    'Subscriber activation workflow: authorized staff or import creates subscriber and service in draft; validation confirms plan, identity, site, payment rule, credential and network dependencies; approved activation creates an entitlement and durable provisioning job; worker and RADIUS policy result are recorded; CoA or Disconnect is issued only when required; verification updates service state; notification is sent. Failed steps enter actionable failed-provisioning state with retry, rollback, and manual resolution options.',
    'Payment-to-access workflow: payment intent is created; provider callback is signature-verified; amount, currency, tenant, invoice or service reference, timestamp, and idempotency key are validated; payment and immutable ledger entry are posted; reconciliation allocates funds; entitlement changes; a network job applies access policy; session action is verified; receipt and notification are delivered. Duplicate, delayed, reversed, disputed, or mismatched callbacks are retained as explicit exceptions and cannot create duplicate service activation.',
    'Support and incident workflow: event, portal user, staff member, email, or approved channel opens a ticket or incident; identity and tenant are resolved; severity and SLA are applied; assignment, internal notes, customer communication, escalation, maintenance link, resolution, reopening, and satisfaction are tracked. NOC can inspect tenant-scoped session and device context; only permitted users can initiate controlled RADIUS actions; support actions create audit events and do not expose secret data.',
    'Access revocation workflow: a membership is disabled in WorkOS; webhook and application mirror update; sessions and scoped tokens are revoked; queued jobs are revalidated at execution; portal and staff routes deny access; audit record is written. Tenant suspension preserves records but blocks tenant mutations, new sessions, provider initiation, and connector commands according to the suspension policy while retaining limited payment and recovery paths.'
